    Kept as ideas, restated in original wording: working memory as the constraint
    that makes readability measurable; deep modules over many shallow ones; that
    line-count rules for functions are the wrong measure; that a little copying
    beats a little dependency; and that layered architectures and DDD building
    blocks are means rather than requirements.

    CHANGES MADE (required by CC BY 4.0): the source is an essay arguing a
    position; this adaptation turns it into a decision rule — where the
    book-derived heuristics above conflict, the reading that leaves less in the
    reader's working memory wins. Its positions were also narrowed: it rejects
    layered architecture and DDD more broadly than this skill does, which keeps
    dependency direction and core-testable-alone as real criteria while dropping
    only the prescribed ceremony. No sentence from either source is reproduced.

    Added, present in neither source: the delete-test as the single criterion,
    abstract-on-the-second-case as the rule, the "when writing rather than
    reviewing" section, and a "do not report" list that explicitly protects
    validation at trust boundaries, intrinsic domain complexity, and exhaustive
    matching over closed sets — guards without which a simplification pass can
    remove code that is load-bearing.
